const e="2026-11-07",t=[{title:"The Data-Centre Dilemma: Powering the Artificial Intelligence Boom",source:"Reuters, 6 November 2026 (Google invests $17 billion in Wyoming data centre; tech sell-off wipes about $2.5 trillion from US stocks; protests against data centres spread across 16 countries)",passage:`The infrastructure of artificial intelligence is, above all, physical. Behind every chatbot answer and image generator stands a warehouse of servers consuming electricity on a scale once associated with small cities. This week, that physical reality collided with politics and financial markets, raising an uncomfortable question: can the world build data centres fast enough — and affordably enough — to sustain the AI boom?

On Thursday, Google announced a $17 billion investment to build its first data centre in Wyoming, acquiring a site in the south-east of the state from AI infrastructure developer Crusoe. The announcement was a statement of intent: despite mounting opposition, the largest technology companies are still expanding. OpenAI, for its part, told CNBC on Friday that it would press ahead with its American data-centre expansion even after a Democratic sweep in the midterm elections fuelled fears of tighter scrutiny. The company also said it would no longer use non-disclosure agreements with public officials when negotiating sites, a concession to critics who accuse the industry of operating in secrecy.

Yet the financial markets told a different story. On Thursday, the sell-off in technology shares wiped roughly $2.5 trillion from the value of American companies, with the Nasdaq Composite falling 5.5% and the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index dropping by about 12% in a single session. Investors are questioning whether the enormous capital expenditure — Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle spent $691 billion on capital projects in the twelve months to September — will ever generate commensurate returns. Credit markets have grown wary too: Dutch lender ING has put about $500 million of data-centre loans up for sale, and ratings agencies have warned that weaker borrowers may struggle to refinance debts falling due between 2027 and 2029. The scale of the borrowing is itself a source of unease. Much of the build-out has been financed with debt raised against projected future revenues rather than current cash flow, a structure that works smoothly while valuations rise but becomes precarious when sentiment turns. Some analysts now draw parallels with earlier infrastructure booms, in which abundant capital encouraged overbuilding and left behind half-empty facilities and stranded assets once demand failed to match the most optimistic forecasts.

Politics has become the other brake on the boom. Tuesday's elections brought dozens of ballot initiatives designed to limit data-centre construction, and in Texas the incoming governor has promised to maintain a freeze on new permits. Democratic governors now oversee states accounting for roughly 80% of the prospective five-year increase in American data-centre power capacity, a dramatic shift from 30% before the vote. A moratorium does not cancel capacity, analysts note, but it displaces it: gigawatts scheduled for 2027 may slip into 2028 or later, pushing up costs and delaying projects.

Opposition is no longer confined to the ballot box. On Friday, protesters broke into the construction site of a Microsoft data centre in Amsterdam, part of a coordinated campaign across 16 countries by the “Stop Dirty Data Centres” coalition. Campaigners argue that the facilities strain electricity and water supplies, compete with housing for scarce land, and jeopardise climate goals. Technology companies counter that data centres are essential infrastructure, and the European Union's tech chief, Henna Virkkunen, said this week that Europe needs more of them, albeit built “with care”.

The industry's defenders point to demand: more than 80% of the record 7.5 gigawatts of data-centre capacity under construction in eight major American markets was already preleased by mid-2026, according to property consultancy CBRE. Builders such as Sweden's Skanska, whose construction orders jumped 70% in the third quarter, are becoming more selective, favouring established technology clients over newer developers. Whether that caution marks a healthy correction or the end of an era of unrestrained expansion will shape not only the technology sector but the economies that have come to depend on it.`,notes:[{word:"infrastructure",pos:"n.",meaning:"基础设施"},{word:"sustain",pos:"v.",meaning:"维持；支撑"},{word:"scrutiny",pos:"n.",meaning:"审查；细察"},{word:"concession",pos:"n.",meaning:"让步；折衷"},{word:"expenditure",pos:"n.",meaning:"开支；花费"},{word:"commensurate",pos:"adj.",meaning:"相称的；相当的"},{word:"wary",pos:"adj.",meaning:"警惕的；谨慎的"},{word:"moratorium",pos:"n.",meaning:"暂停；延期"},{word:"prospective",pos:"adj.",meaning:"预期的；未来的"},{word:"displace",pos:"v.",meaning:"使偏移；取代"},{word:"coordinated",pos:"adj.",meaning:"协调的；统一安排的"},{word:"strain",pos:"v.",meaning:"使超负荷；紧张"},{word:"jeopardise",pos:"v.",meaning:"危及；使危险"},{word:"preleased",pos:"adj.",meaning:"已预租出的"}],questions:[{q:"What is the main idea of the passage?",options:["Google's investment in Wyoming will solve the data-centre shortage","The AI data-centre boom is facing financial, political and social pressures at the same time","Protests against data centres have forced technology companies to abandon their plans","The semiconductor industry is in a permanent decline"],answer:1},{q:"Google's $17 billion investment in Wyoming shows that",options:["large technology companies are retreating from data-centre construction","the biggest technology companies are still expanding despite opposition","Google has abandoned its partnership with Crusoe","Wyoming has banned all new data centres"],answer:1},{q:"OpenAI said it would halt its US data-centre expansion after the midterm elections.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"More than 80% of the record 7.5 gigawatts of data-centre capacity under construction in eight major US markets had already been preleased by mid-2026.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"Why did technology shares fall sharply on Thursday?",options:["Because Google cancelled its Wyoming data-centre project","Because investors doubted whether huge capital spending would produce matching returns","Because the US government banned all AI investment","Because data-centre electricity prices fell"],answer:1},{q:"The incoming governor of Texas has promised to lift the freeze on new data-centre permits.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"According to the passage, what is the effect of a moratorium on data-centre construction?",options:["It permanently cancels planned capacity","It delays and displaces capacity to later years","It reduces electricity demand immediately","It has no effect on construction schedules"],answer:1}]},{title:"Antalya Awaits: The Climate Summit That Cannot Afford to Fail",source:"Carbon Brief, 4 November 2026 (COP31 opens 9 November in Antalya; COP17 concludes with first global review of 2030 nature targets; Brazil releases roadmap to end deforestation)",passage:`On Monday 9 November, negotiators from nearly 200 countries will gather in Antalya, Türkiye, for COP31, the 31st Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. The summit, which runs until 20 November under the presidency of Turkish Environment Minister Murat Kurum, arrives at a moment of acute tension: the scientific evidence of warming grows more alarming each year, while the political consensus required to act on it appears ever more fragile.

The omens are mixed. In Yerevan, Armenia, the COP17 nature talks concluded in the early hours of the morning with what delegates described as a landmark achievement: the first global review of progress towards the 2030 nature targets, including the goal of conserving at least 30% of the world's ecosystems. The Commonwealth has pledged $202 million to help middle- and low-income countries expand protected areas and conserve fragile habitats that might otherwise be lost to development, and a new study found that at least 107,356 species are used or traded globally — more than double previous estimates, a finding that underscores how much remains unknown about humanity's impact on the natural world.

On forests, the picture is starker. On 3 November, Brazil's presidency of the COP30 climate talks published a long-awaited roadmap for ending deforestation, estimating that $300 billion a year will be needed by 2030 to halt and reverse forest loss, while current finance reaches less than one-third of that figure. The roadmap divides action into three parts — national policy, international cooperation and financial mechanisms — and offers ten proposals, from aligning domestic finance flows to strengthening the position of forests in carbon markets. Whether governments will follow it voluntarily remains an open question.

The urgency is not abstract. In September, a surge in forest and land fires in Indonesia pushed air pollution to unprecedented levels and caused an estimated 3,560 deaths, according to the Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air. In Antarctica, meanwhile, the Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources ended its latest meeting without agreement on a new fisheries management plan for krill or a new marine protected area, despite what observers called high expectations.

Against this backdrop, COP31's negotiators, led in the negotiating rooms by Australia's Chris Bowen as President of Negotiations, face a familiar dilemma: how to convert pledges into policies that survive contact with domestic politics. Previous summits have shown that ambitious language is the easy part; the hard part is finance, enforcement and the willingness of major emitters to accept binding constraints. The Antalya talks will also host the eighth meeting of the Parties to the Paris Agreement and the twenty-first meeting of the Parties to the Kyoto Protocol, giving delegates a crowded agenda. Small island states, meanwhile, continue to press for dedicated finance to adapt to rising seas, arguing that they contributed least to the problem yet face its most immediate consequences. Their demands have become a moral touchstone of the negotiations: if the summit cannot deliver for those on the front line of climate change, delegates warn, its credibility with the wider world will suffer.

Optimists note that the architecture of climate diplomacy has survived far worse, and that the first global stocktake of nature targets demonstrates that agreement is still possible. Pessimists reply that stocktakes and roadmaps are not substitutes for action, and that each year of delay raises the eventual cost — in money, in ecosystems and in lives. When the gavel falls in Antalya on 20 November, the measure of success will not be the warmth of the closing speeches but whether the world leaves with credible plans to close the gap between what has been promised and what has been delivered.`,notes:[{word:"negotiator",pos:"n.",meaning:"谈判代表"},{word:"fragile",pos:"adj.",meaning:"脆弱的；不稳固的"},{word:"landmark",pos:"adj.",meaning:"里程碑式的"},{word:"conserve",pos:"v.",meaning:"保护；保存"},{word:"underscore",pos:"v.",meaning:"强调；突显"},{word:"stark",pos:"adj.",meaning:"严峻的；全然的"},{word:"halt",pos:"v.",meaning:"停止；制止"},{word:"mechanism",pos:"n.",meaning:"机制；机构"},{word:"unprecedented",pos:"adj.",meaning:"前所未有的"},{word:"dilemma",pos:"n.",meaning:"进退两难的境地"},{word:"binding",pos:"adj.",meaning:"具有约束力的"},{word:"constraint",pos:"n.",meaning:"限制；约束"},{word:"stocktake",pos:"n.",meaning:"盘点；全面评估"},{word:"credible",pos:"adj.",meaning:"可信的；可靠的"}],questions:[{q:"What is the main purpose of the passage?",options:["To predict the exact outcome of the COP31 negotiations","To explain the background, challenges and stakes of the COP31 climate summit","To criticise the Turkish government for hosting COP31","To describe the wildlife of Antalya, Türkiye"],answer:1},{q:"What was described as a landmark achievement of the COP17 nature talks?",options:["A ban on all wildlife trade","The first global review of progress towards the 2030 nature targets","An agreement to end deforestation by 2030","A new marine protected area in Antarctica"],answer:1},{q:"Current finance for halting deforestation is less than one-third of the $300 billion a year needed by 2030.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:0},{q:"The Antarctic meeting agreed on a new marine protected area.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"Why does the author mention the fires in Indonesia?",options:["To show that the urgency of climate action is real and measured in human lives","To argue that Indonesia should host the next climate summit","To explain how forest fires start in tropical countries","To suggest that air pollution is no longer a problem in Asia"],answer:0},{q:"COP31 is chaired by Australia's Chris Bowen.",options:["True","False","Not Given"],answer:1},{q:"According to the final paragraph, how should the success of COP31 be judged?",options:["By the number of countries that attend the summit","By whether the world leaves with credible plans to close the gap between promises and delivery","By the warmth of the closing speeches","By the length of the final agreement"],answer:1}]}],a={date:e,newsReadings:t};export{e as date,a as default,t as newsReadings};
